synthesis.summary:
Yes, the idea can be built, and it is the right model, provided "group" is read one specific way. A roll's group is every row whose tags do not clash with any tag already rolled: the dials, every earlier row, the earlier draw of the same table, and earlier phases. The arbiter works that group out at the moment of the draw. That is exactly the owner's "the earlier tags are the next roll's headings". It is also how design_arbiter.arbitrate() already works: it filters the pool before the die against everything rolled. The lifeline-by-palette, rule-by-ruin and action-by-target chains already work this way and never contradict themselves.

Two literal readings fail:
- Physical sub-tables per heading. Rows would be copied, the unique-id rule breaks, and used.json would repeat one idea under two ids.
- One heading per roll. The trope break clashes with five different parents (dials 62%, main contest 19%, spine 14%, second contest 6%, lifeline 4%). A heading tree for it needs about 295 combinations (1,470 possible).

Read as "compatible with all earlier tags", the idea removes every audited contradiction. Births holding one go from 4.9-5.7% (7.2% on the extended set) to 0 in 3,000 seeds and across all 45 scale x magic x era combinations. No pool runs empty, and trope entropy goes from 5.040 to 5.036 bits (the maximum is 5.044).

The part not to take literally: turning today's soft affinities into hard "only inside your heading" groups. That shrinks the key tables 4-7x, leaves 4% of births with an empty pool, and makes 26% of same-contest-family campaigns share a trope break. That is the repetition the owner dislikes. So:
- tags are hard where two rows' sentences contradict literally;
- positive headings stay hard only where they already work (a specific row of a large, rotating table);
- theme stays a soft weight.

This is not a rewrite. The tables stay, and rows gain tags. The new machinery is small:
- a closed tag registry file;
- a `claims` field on rows and on dial values;
- about 70 lines that turn claims into tokens the arbiter already understands (arbitrate() is unchanged);
- enforcing families_distinct;
- two usage fixes.

Limits to state plainly:
- Tags guarantee only what they name. Completeness comes from reviewing each topic against each table.
- The prose (the writer and the sentence templates) and the P2+ prerolls must use the same tags.
- A later table always yields to an earlier one.

The right moment is before build item 10: the identity roller is not coded yet, and it can use tags instead of four more ad hoc mechanisms.

holes.example:
scratchpad/seq.py, 20 sequences x 40 births, prohibition draw taken last:
- group size with R7: 3 rows in 10 of 75 taboo draws, 4 rows in 20 (40% at 4 or fewer);
- without claims: 4 rows in 16%;
- 55% of taboo draws fall back to used rows.
